{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BH6x68w1RECuPX2tUmw76H","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Effort and composition gate\n2\t\n3\tTask-Spec v3 uses six sizes and two kinds of unit. Size is a decomposition\n4\tsignal, not a vendor ranking.\n5\t\n6\t| Size | Kind | Write-surface budget | Rule |\n7\t|---|---|---:|---|\n8\t| XS | Leaf | 1 | Directly runnable |\n9\t| S | Leaf | 2 | Directly runnable |\n10\t| M | Leaf | 3 | Directly runnable |\n11\t| L | Leaf | 5 | Directly runnable only on a configured long-horizon backend and with one coherent done-condition |\n12\t| XL | Node | 0 | At least two child Task-Spec ids; never delegated |\n13\t| XXL | Node | 0 | At least three child Task-Spec ids; never delegated |\n14\t\n15\tThe write surface is `touches_paths ∪ creates_paths`, counted uniquely. A leaf\n16\tabove its budget warns because the declared atom is probably coarse. A node with\n17\ta write path fails because the children—not their composition parent—own work.\n18\t\n19\t## Long-horizon backends\n20\t\n21\t`execution_backend` remains an open string. The default L-eligible set is:\n22\t\n23\t```text\n24\tglm claude codex kimi\n25\t```\n26\t\n27\tAn installation may change it without changing the format:\n28\t\n29\t```bash\n30\texport TASKSPEC_LONG_HORIZON_BACKENDS=\"claude codex kimi glm my-harness\"\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tThe rule is capability-based: an L leaf needs a long-horizon builder. The core\n34\tdoes not claim that one current model vendor is universally best.\n35\t\n36\t## Composition is still Task-Spec\n37\t\n38\t```mermaid\n39\tflowchart TD\n40\t    XXL[\"XXL node\"] --> XL[\"XL node\"]\n41\t    XXL --> L[\"L leaf\"]\n42\t    XL --> M[\"M leaf\"]\n43\t    XL --> S[\"S leaf\"]\n44\t    M --> XS[\"XS leaf\"]\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\tAn XL/XXL node is a reviewable decomposition and composition unit. It names\n48\tchildren but is not a runnable leaf, cannot be sealed for delegation, and has no\n49\twrite surface. A worker receives a child handoff; a higher-level system may\n50\tcompose child outcomes back into the node.\n51\t\n52\t## Classification questions\n53\t\n54\tClassify from the actual repository, not estimated elapsed time alone:\n55\t\n56\t1. How many existing and new paths may be written?\n57\t2. Do the reads, spec, and likely diff fit in one fresh executor context?\n58\t3. Is there one coherent machine-checkable done-condition?\n59\t4. Are multiple outcomes independently valuable or independently reversible?\n60\t5. Does a wide refactor need expand → migrate batches → contract leaves?\n61\t\n62\tIf multiple outcomes can succeed or fail independently, split them and connect\n63\tthe leaves with `depends_on`. If a task is large because its design is still\n64\tunknown, use the appropriate design process first, then express approved build\n65\tunits as Task-Spec leaves/nodes.\n66\t\n67\t## Related\n68\t\n69\t- [Decomposition](decomposition.md)\n70\t- [Profiles](profiles.md)\n71\t- [Task-Spec v3](../../spec/task-spec-v3.md)\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc5e72fb-6966-4d12-81ea-3be43c3807ef","uuid":"519d7ca0-a2a2-4ee3-a09e-8058b9bd94f8","timestamp":"2026-09-15T10:58:34.591Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat s9gcd_51/.agents/skills/task-spec/references/concepts/effort-gate.md","content":"# Effort and composition gate\n\nTask-Spec v3 uses six sizes and two kinds of unit. Size is a decomposition\nsignal, not a vendor ranking.\n\n| Size | Kind | Write-surface budget | Rule |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| XS | Leaf | 1 | Directly runnable |\n| S | Leaf | 2 | Directly runnable |\n| M | Leaf | 3 | Directly runnable |\n| L | Leaf | 5 | Directly runnable only on a configured long-horizon backend and with one coherent done-condition |\n| XL | Node | 0 | At least two child Task-Spec ids; never delegated |\n| XXL | Node | 0 | At least three child Task-Spec ids; never delegated |\n\nThe write surface is `touches_paths ∪ creates_paths`, counted uniquely. A leaf\nabove its budget warns because the declared atom is probably coarse. A node with\na write path fails because the children—not their composition parent—own work.\n\n## Long-horizon backends\n\n`execution_backend` remains an open string. The default L-eligible set is:\n\n```text\nglm claude codex kimi\n```\n\nAn installation may change it without changing the format:\n\n```bash\nexport TASKSPEC_LONG_HORIZON_BACKENDS=\"claude codex kimi glm my-harness\"\n```\n\nThe rule is capability-based: an L leaf needs a long-horizon builder. The core\ndoes not claim that one current model vendor is universally best.\n\n## Composition is still Task-Spec\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    XXL[\"XXL node\"] --> XL[\"XL node\"]\n    XXL --> L[\"L leaf\"]\n    XL --> M[\"M leaf\"]\n    XL --> S[\"S leaf\"]\n    M --> XS[\"XS leaf\"]\n```\n\nAn XL/XXL node is a reviewable decomposition and composition unit. It names\nchildren but is not a runnable leaf, cannot be sealed for delegation, and has no\nwrite surface. A worker receives a child handoff; a higher-level system may\ncompose child outcomes back into the node.\n\n## Classification questions\n\nClassify from the actual repository, not estimated elapsed time alone:\n\n1. How many existing and new paths may be written?\n2. Do the reads, spec, and likely diff fit in one fresh executor context?\n3. Is there one coherent machine-checkable done-condition?\n4. Are multiple outcomes independently valuable or independently reversible?\n5. Does a wide refactor need expand → migrate batches → contract leaves?\n\nIf multiple outcomes can succeed or fail independently, split them and connect\nthe leaves with `depends_on`. If a task is large because its design is still\nunknown, use the appropriate design process first, then express approved build\nunits as Task-Spec leaves/nodes.\n\n## Related\n\n- [Decomposition](decomposition.md)\n- [Profiles](profiles.md)\n- [Task-Spec v3](../../spec/task-spec-v3.md)\n","numLines":72,"startLine":1,"totalLines":72}}}
